solution:
Negative powers mean reciprocals: \(a^{-n}=1/a^n\) for \(a\ne0\). The original expression requires \(a\ne0\), \(b\ne0\), and \(a^{-3}-b^{-3}\ne0\). First combine the denominator:
\[
a^{-3}-b^{-3}
=\frac1{a^3}-\frac1{b^3}
=\frac{b^3-a^3}{a^3b^3}.
\]
Thus \(b^3-a^3\ne0\). Dividing by this fraction means multiplying by its reciprocal, so
\[
\frac{a^{-1}b^{-1}}{a^{-3}-b^{-3}}
=\frac1{ab}\cdot\frac{a^3b^3}{b^3-a^3}
=\frac{a^2b^2}{b^3-a^3}.
\]
This equality retains the original restrictions on \(a\) and \(b\). The answer is choice B.

